21:44:46 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: It’s understandable to wonder, but you don’t need to assume he’ll misbehave just because he’s a boy. Children vary much more as individuals than by gender, and starting school is a big adjustment for many of them. Some early-school behaviors—fidgeting, talking out of turn, difficulty sharing, big emotions, or testing limits—are common developmental experiences for children of all genders. Teachers expect this and usually have routines to help children learn classroom skills gradually. You can support him by: - Talking positively about school and what a typical day may be like. - Practising simple routines: listening when someone speaks, asking for help, taking turns, packing his bag. - Naming feelings and discussing calm ways to handle frustration. - Making sure he gets enough sleep, food, active play, and predictable routines. - Keeping in touch with his teacher early on if you have concerns. Rather than worrying in advance, watch how he settles in and respond to the specific support he needs.
