21:45:12 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Your pediatrician is broadly reflecting a real finding: on average, girls and boys can differ somewhat in the timing of development in certain early-childhood skills, including language, self-regulation, and some aspects of executive function. In many studies, girls as a group show an earlier average advantage in those areas during the preschool/early-school years. But “average” is the important word. The ranges overlap enormously: many boys are ahead of many girls in readiness, attention, language, and emotional regulation. Brain development also does not proceed on one simple “mature/slower” scale—children can be advanced in one area and need support in another. So it is reasonable to expect that your son may need time and practice with sitting, transitions, impulse control, and classroom routines. It would not be reasonable to conclude in advance that he will be disruptive or unable to cope because he is a boy. The useful middle ground is: - Prepare and practise classroom skills at home. - Choose a school/classroom that includes movement, play, and clear routines. - Check in with the teacher after the first few weeks. - Take persistent difficulties seriously rather than writing them off as “normal for boys”—especially if they interfere substantially with learning, friendships, or daily life. In short: sex-related developmental averages can be relevant context, but your son’s own temperament and development are much better guides to what support he may need.
