21:45:30 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It’s a very common belief, and it reflects patterns people may genuinely have observed. In early childhood, boys as a group may show later average development in some skills that classrooms demand—such as impulse control, sustained attention, and language-based self-regulation. The problem is treating that group-level tendency as a rule about boys’ character or capability. “Boys mature slower” is too broad to explain any individual child, and it can become self-fulfilling if adults expect less self-control from boys or excuse behavior they would address in girls. A fairer, more useful framing is: **some children need more time, movement, practice, and structure to develop classroom-regulation skills; your son may or may not be one of them.** You can hold realistic expectations while still teaching him that he is capable of listening, being kind, taking turns, and managing his body in class. If you notice concerns, focus on what is happening—e.g., difficulty with transitions, sitting through stories, frustration, or following directions—and work with his teacher on specific supports.
