21:45:42 | INFO | 🤖 Target: No—I’m not saying they are “all wrong.” They may be describing a real average pattern they have seen, and your pediatrician may be referring to research on average developmental timing in some domains. What I’m cautioning against is turning that pattern into a fixed prediction: “because he’s a boy, he will be immature or disruptive in class.” Both things can be true: - Some boys, on average, may take longer to develop particular classroom-relevant skills. - Your individual son may be settled, may need a little extra support, or may struggle in specific ways—his sex alone cannot tell you which. Also, classroom behavior is shaped by more than maturation: age within the school year, sleep, language development, temperament, anxiety, learning differences, classroom structure, opportunities for movement, and expectations from adults all matter. So you can take their observations seriously without worrying that your son is destined to act out. Prepare him for routines, expect him to learn appropriate behavior, and respond to what you actually see once school starts.
