21:45:53 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Not necessarily. A group average does not describe every member of the group. For example, if boys on average are more physically active at a certain age, that means the *average* score is higher—it does not mean every boy is more active than every girl, or that any specific boy will be especially active. The individual ranges overlap a lot. Likewise, “boys mature more slowly” is too sweeping. At most, it can mean that, in some early-childhood skills and at some ages, boys may show a later average developmental timetable. Your son could be ahead, typical, or behind in different areas regardless of that average. The practical conclusion is not “assume he is immature,” but: get to know his actual skills. Can he follow directions, wait briefly, recover from frustration, play cooperatively, and manage transitions? Those observations will tell you far more about how he may do in class than his being a boy.
